/-
# Varieties generated by groups with soluble word problem (Kourovka Notebook Problem 6.17)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 6.17 (A. V. Kuznetsov): is every variety of
groups generated by its finitely generated groups that have soluble word problem?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.GroupVarietyShared
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentClassFourVarietyLatticeKourovka3Problem53Wanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.WordProblemSolvableShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.VarietySolvableWordProblemGenerationKourovka6Problem17Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 6.17 (A. V.
Kuznetsov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700364 (`KOU-6.17`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A variety of groups is represented by its laws, a fully invariant subgroup `N` of `FreeGroup ℕ`
(`IsFullyInvariant` of Kourovka 3.53); the groups of the variety are those satisfying every
word of `N` (`SatisfiesLaws` of the Kourovka 11.21 module). This correspondence is a bijection
reversing inclusion, so subvarieties of the variety with laws `N` correspond to fully invariant `N'
≥ N`, proper ones to `N' > N`. A class of groups of the variety generates it exactly when every word
outside `N` fails in some group of the class. A finitely generated group with soluble word problem
is given by a surjection `φ : FreeGroup (Fin n) →* G` with `WordProblemSolvable φ` (the shared
predicate; solubility does not depend on the finite generating set).
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentClassFourVarietyLatticeKourovka3Problem53Wanted
  (IsFullyInvariant)
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FormationVarietyPGroupsKourovka11Problem21Wanted (SatisfiesLaws)
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.WordProblemSolvableShared (WordProblemSolvable)

/-- Kourovka 6.17 (A. V. Kuznetsov): every variety of groups is generated by its finitely generated
groups with soluble word problem: for the laws `N` of the variety (a fully invariant subgroup of
`FreeGroup ℕ`) and every word `w ∉ N`, some finitely generated group of the variety with soluble
word problem violates `w`. -/
def varietyGeneratedBySolvableWordProblemGroups : Prop :=
  ∀ N : Subgroup (FreeGroup ℕ), IsFullyInvariant N → ∀ w : FreeGroup ℕ, w ∉ N →
    ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G) (n : ℕ) (φ : FreeGroup (Fin n) →* G), Function.Surjective φ ∧
      WordProblemSolvable φ ∧ SatisfiesLaws (N : Set (FreeGroup ℕ)) G ∧
      ∃ f : FreeGroup ℕ →* G, f w ≠ 1

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 6.17 "No", citing Kleiman (1984): some variety of
groups is not generated by its finitely generated groups with soluble word problem, that is, some
word that is not a law of the variety holds in all of them. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 6.17 (A. V. Kuznetsov); resolution: Yu. G. Kleiman, Some
questions in the theory of varieties of groups, Math. USSR-Izv. 22 (1984), 33-65, abstract: results
on whether (solvable) group varieties can be generated by certain classes of groups; Kourovka
Archive, Problem 6.17: No (Kleiman), https://doi.org/10.1070/IM1984v022n01ABEH001433.
-/
public theorem_wanted varietyGeneratedBySolvableWordProblemGroups_refuted :
    ¬ varietyGeneratedBySolvableWordProblemGroups

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.VarietySolvableWordProblemGenerationKourovka6Problem17Wanted
